# DSH plugin-install probe — the NPM install route, measured end to end
#
#   node deepblend/tools/dsh-plugin-install-probe.mjs --spec '@deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle'
#
# Run with an EMPTY `npm_config_store_dir` (see the tarball log for why) and with
# `npm_config_registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/`, because this machine's configured registry is
# a mirror and a mirror does not carry a package published minutes ago.
#
# Taken 2026-09-23, minutes after the 0.2.1 release, with the same criteria applied to all three
# routes — that sameness is the point of `--spec`, and the criteria are:
#
#   installed version   the bundle the profile actually got, and the six exact pins it carries
#   workbench route     `GET /deepblend/workbench` — the route ONLY the current version has, and
#                       the reason a version number alone is not enough: a stale artifact states
#                       its own version truthfully, while a route it does not have answers 404
#   presets             both deployed, byte-identical to `deepblend/presets/`, `problem: null`
#
# What it is evidence for: the route the plugin list RECOMMENDS, and the one the release chain
# had left behind. Until the 0.2.0 release this route served 0.1.0 — a build made before the
# standalone workbench existed — while `main` served `/deepblend/workbench` with 200.
#
# The reading that separates this route from the other two is `packages pnpm fetched`: 7 here,
# because the seven packages come from the registry and none of them goes back to git. That is not
# automatic — the repository's manifests carry self-referential `github:` specs because the SOURCE
# route needs them — so `tools/publish-packages.mjs` rewrites them to exact versions in a staged
# copy, and `installed sibling pins` above is the read-back that says it did: six exact `0.2.0`,
# zero git specs.
#
# Body is the tool's output.
